## Why This Is Asked

Auditors require end-to-end data provenance in KCNA with tenant isolation, cryptographic integrity, and scalable verification. This question tests how to design lightweight, verifiable lineage without payload leakage and how to handle dynamic rotation and maintenance under failure.

## Key Concepts

- Data provenance and per-tenant isolation
- Cryptographic hash chaining and Merkle trees
- Envelope encryption and retention policies
- At-least-once semantics and failure recovery

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight Merkle root builder (conceptual)
class MerkleTree {
  constructor(){ this.leaves = []; this.rootHash = null; }
  addLeaf(data) { this.leaves.push(hash(data)); this.rootHash = this.computeRoot(); }
  computeRoot(){ // simplistic binary Merkle root placeholder
    let nodes = this.leaves.slice();
    while (nodes.length > 1) {
      const next = [];
      for (let i = 0; i < nodes.length; i += 2) {
        const a = nodes[i];
        const b = nodes[i+1] || a;
        next.push(hash(a + b));
      }
      nodes = next;
    }
    return nodes[0] || null;
  }
}
function hash(x){ // placeholder for a real crypto hash
  return require('crypto').createHash('sha256').update(x).digest('hex');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale Merkle trees across thousands of tenants without cross-tenant leakage?
- How do you rotate keys without invalidating existing proofs, and what is the audit protocol for retroactive verifications?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner KCNA candidate's ability to implement a time-window join across topics, with late data, offset handling, and at-least-once guarantees. It requires simple in-memory state management, per-key routing, and clear API boundaries.

## Key Concepts

- Time-window joins across topics
- Late-arriving data handling
- Per-message offset tracking
- Idempotent emissions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch for in-memory join
class JoinEnricher {
  constructor() {
    this.latestAnnotation = new Map(); // serviceId -> { annotation, ts }
    this.metricBuffer = new Map(); // serviceId -> [{metric, ts}]
  }
  processMetric(metric, offset, ts) {
    const ann = this.latestAnnotation.get(metric.serviceId);
    const enriched = ann ? { ...metric, annotation: ann.annotation } : { ...metric, annotation: null };
    this.emitEnriched(enriched, offset);
  }
  processAnnotation(ann, offset, ts) {
    this.latestAnnotation.set(ann.serviceId, { annotation: ann.value, ts });
    // re-emit any buffered metrics for this service
    const buffered = this.metricBuffer.get(ann.serviceId) || [];
    for (const m of buffered) {
      this.emitEnriched({ ...m.metric, annotation: ann.value }, m.offset);
    }
    this.metricBuffer.set(ann.serviceId, []);
  }
  emitEnriched(record, offset) {
    // send to metrics_enriched and commit offset afterward
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical TTL-state reasoning and side-input-driven re-enrichment under at-least-once guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- TTL cache per merchant
- Side-input rule versioning
- Exactly-once-like semantics via idempotent emits and commitOffsets
- Re-enrichment on rule updates

## Code Example
```javascript
function enrich(txn, ctx) {
  const rule = cache.get(txn.merchantId);
  if (!rule || rule.expiry < now()) {
    emit({ ...txn, riskScore: null, ruleVersion: rule?.ruleVersion ?? 0 });
  } else {
    emit({ ...txn, riskScore: rule.riskScore, ruleVersion: rule.ruleVersion });
  }
}
```

